---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../Components/Header/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../Components/Footer/Footer.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../constants';

const updated = 'August 28, 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy — Jason Shprintz"
  description="What jasonshprintz.com collects, who receives it, and how to avoid it."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="policy" aria-label="Privacy policy">
    <span class="kicker">Privacy</span>
    <h1>What this site collects</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This policy covers <strong>jasonshprintz.com</strong> and nothing else. It is
      my personal site. Projects linked from here — GitAll, Alley Admin, Dynastiest
      League, Toastbyte Studios — are separate and have their own terms.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      There are no accounts, no forms, no newsletter and no ads. I do not sell
      anything and I do not sell data. The site measures how many people visit
      and which pages they read, and that is the extent of it.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookie consent</h2>
    <p>
      Because Google Analytics 4 sets a non-essential cookie, the site asks for
      your permission before it loads. A banner appears the first time you
      visit. You can accept or decline — both choices are given equal weight,
      and the site works exactly the same either way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing analytics-related loads until you answer. If you close the page
      without choosing, that counts as a decline and no analytics cookie is set.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your choice is stored in a cookie named <code>analytics-consent</code>,
      valid for one year. To change your mind at any time, use the{' '}
      <strong>Cookie settings</strong> button in the footer. A new choice replaces
      the old one immediately.
    </p>

    <h3>The cookies involved</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>analytics-consent</code> — set by this site, records whether you accepted
        or declined, kept for one year
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cf_consent</code> or <code>zaraz-consent</code> — Cloudflare's tag manager
        keeps its own record of the same decision, so that it knows whether it is
        allowed to run the analytics tag
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cfz_google-analytics_v4</code> and{' '}
        <code>cfzs_google-analytics_v4</code> — the Google Analytics identifiers.
        These are set <strong>only if you accept</strong>. If you decline before
        accepting, they are not set.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you go looking, there is no <code>_ga</code> cookie on this site. That is
      the name Google Analytics normally uses, but because the tag runs at Cloudflare's
      edge rather than in your browser, the equivalent identifiers are the <code
        >cfz_</code
      > pair above. Its absence does not mean analytics is switched off.
    </p>

    <h2>Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      The site uses Google Analytics 4, loaded through Cloudflare Zaraz. Zaraz
      injects the tag at Cloudflare's edge rather than from a script in the
      page, so the requests are first-party, but the data still goes to Google.
    </p>
    <p>What that records on a visit:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>which pages you opened, when, and how long you stayed</li>
      <li>the page or search that referred you</li>
      <li>
        an approximate location — usually country and region — derived from your
        IP address
      </li>
      <li>your browser, operating system and rough screen size</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Google Analytics 4 does not store your full IP address. It sets the
      first-party cookies named above so that a second visit from the same
      browser is counted as a returning visitor rather than a new one.
    </p>

    <h3>How long it is kept</h3>
    <p>
      The detailed record of a visit — the individual events and their
      parameters — is deleted by Google after <strong>two months</strong>. The
      user-level record, the thing that lets two visits from the same browser be
      recognised as one returning visitor, is deleted after
      <strong>fourteen months</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      One honest caveat: those windows govern the detailed and user-level data.
      Aggregate totals — how many people read a page in a given month — sit in
      Google's standard reports and are not deleted on that schedule.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this tells me who you are. I see counts and patterns, not people.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by Cloudflare Pages. Cloudflare processes every request
      to deliver the page and to protect the site from abuse, which involves
      handling your IP address and may involve setting its own cookies for bot
      detection. That happens at the network level, before anything I control.
      Cloudflare's handling is governed by its own privacy terms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloudflare also rewrites the email addresses on this site to make them
      harder for scrapers to harvest, which is why the contact links look
      unusual in the page source.
    </p>

    <h2>Things loaded from elsewhere</h2>
    <p>
      Two parts of this page are fetched from other servers, and those servers
      see your IP address as a consequence of serving the request:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Fonts</strong> — the two typefaces this site uses, Cormorant
        Garamond and Lora, are requested from{' '}
        <code>fonts.googleapis.com</code> and <code>fonts.gstatic.com</code>.
        Google receives your IP address and basic request details as part of
        serving them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitAll</strong> — the contribution heatmap is an image served from
        gitall.app, a tool I built and run
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Neither of these sets a cookie, which is why neither is covered by the
      consent banner. That distinction is worth stating plainly rather than
      leaving you to infer it: <strong
        >declining analytics does not stop the font requests</strong
      >. They happen on every page load, before any choice is made, and they go
      to Google regardless of what you chose.
    </p>
    <p>
      A content blocker will stop them. So would my serving the fonts from this
      domain instead of Google's, which removes the third party from the picture
      entirely — that is on my list to do.
    </p>

    <h2>If you email me</h2>
    <p>
      Then I have your email address and whatever you wrote, in my inbox, for as
      long as I keep the message. That is ordinary correspondence rather than
      anything the site collected.
    </p>

    <h2>Avoiding the analytics</h2>
    <p>
      Decline in the consent banner and no analytics cookie is set. The{' '}
      <strong>Cookie settings</strong> button in the footer lets you change that choice
      at any time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Any content blocker or privacy-focused browser will also stop the
      analytics from loading, as will Google's official opt-out browser add-on.
      The site works normally without it. A blocker has the added effect of
      stopping the font requests described above, which the consent banner does
      not govern.
    </p>
    <p>
      Worth being straight about the limits of asking me instead: the analytics
      data is not linked to a name or an email, so I have no reliable way to
      find your records and delete them on request. Blocking it is the remedy
      that actually works. If you want to ask anyway, write to me and I will
      tell you what I can.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If what the site collects changes, this page changes with it and the date
      above moves. There is no mailing list to notify.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about any of this go to&nbsp;
      <a href={LINKS.email}>jshprintz@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding: 72px 0 24px;
  }

  .kicker {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 42%, transparent);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 12px 0 24px;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 44px);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.12;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 48px 0 12px;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 21px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 28px 0 12px;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 17px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 17px;
  }

  .updated {
    font-family:
      ui-monospace,
      SFMono-Regular,
      SF Mono,
      Menlo,
      monospace;
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 42%, transparent);
  }

  p,
  li {
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 78%, transparent);
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    padding-left: 20px;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }

  strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  code {
    font-family:
      ui-monospace,
      SFMono-Regular,
      SF Mono,
      Menlo,
      monospace;
    font-size: 0.875em;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
</style>
